cora's vocal journey was one that i  really really really sat with  with my dialet coach it wasn't just  about sounding like  a person in the times but shaping her  entire story based on the fact that this  is a story of  africans in america and not just african  americans  you know and so on top of that it was  working through her emotional journey  her physical journey and her mental  journey  the one thing that i really appreciated  i think after watching the first  if not the first two episodes one of my  south african  friends was like in complete shock and  the first thing she said was  i didn't know that this is what african  americans went through  and she's lived in america for over 10  years for more than 10 years  and that's the one thing that i realized  upon interacting with the material  and it's something that i think most of  south africa or you know africa a large  uh will appreciate or come to understand  is that we think we know  but then when you when you interact or  engage with the material you realize  that you actually  have no clue and so going into this  process it was important for me to  to come with that understanding that  i cannot dictate or i cannot impose my  ideas  of what i think the story is about i had  to completely open up myself  to learning anew from scratch and  it was barry had sent me a lot of  material to consume  reading material audio tapes and it was  in the audio tapes that something  really stuck out to me it wasn't hearing  the the former enslaved people share  their testimony  that something in me shifted  and went i actually have  no clue at all what it is that what it  is that i'm about to  to um to tell you know and  in that and it was one it's it's layered  in that just hearing them brought out  these  these layers for me in the sense that  one i am listening  to people who are above the age of 75  yet they sound 16. um the way that they  speak they speak a  very broken english that even when um  going through  the the written testimonies with my  dialect coach  i couldn't make sense of what was  happening of what was being shared until  i  i would say it out loud so i could hear  it and it  it it made me understand that the  english that they were speaking  was not english for conversation but  english instruction which is what we  don't see  in hollywood movies today so my  perception of the african-american body  in the 1800s was completely wrong up  until that point i remember i even spoke  to barry about it  and i said barry  they they don't sound like you know  what we think they sound so how do we  find that middle  ground and for me it was really really  important and as much as  we don't want to speak in a way that  would require us to have subtitles  throughout the  story but i was like i cannot totally  disregard  the type of english that they spoke  because it places us  in the space that they were in and also  um  then i i was reminded of  a course that i did in university under  my physical theater course  um part of the curriculum was voice  movement therapy  which is a course that helped us  understand and track  what the voice holds and the certain  traumas that can  shape how you sound in your everyday  life and so for me  korra who comes up as a girl all she  knows is the  life in the plantation she has her  mother and then one day her mother is  not there  and then at some point she is gang raped  by a group of men on the plantation who  then  go out and start rumors about how she  she sleeps with animals and she houses  the moon  and so as a result you know she would  naturally isolate  because now she's ostracized by her  community  and on top of all these other brutal and  traumatizing things that just come with  the life that she had  that she was birthed into you know and  so for me it was important to understand  or showcase or track  how that sounds vocally because again  korra hardly ever speaks  so this chaos and turmoil that is  internal  what does that look like what does that  sound like when she eventually does  open her mouth and as we see when we get  up to um episode nine  uh episode eight and episode nine this  whole idea of have you shared your  testimony  you know so and sharing that testament  means that she has to open her mouth and  speak  so cora's vocal journey was one that i  really really really sat with with my  dialet coach it wasn't just about  sounding like a person in the times  but shaping her entire story based on  the fact that this is a story of  africans in america and not just african  americans  you know and so on top of that it was  working through her emotional journey  her physical journey and her mental  journey  but what really pushed me through was  core's vocal journey  more than anything and on top of  that just understanding her building her  i took a lot of  the stuff that even if it wasn't on the  script  we found in the book and i carried it  over  two book layers of who korra was i have  a lot of notes like  on my personal notes in in building the  character where i would literally put  the page number  of the book so that i could go back and  find that moment so i understand what it  is that i'm doing in that moment in the  scene  well it all came it all came through um  i  i think of nina simone performances  where i'm like my god is she is she  is she giving too much um and and that's  that's what i  honestly i'm being honest and i i know  that barry had um  mental health practitioners and  therapists for everyone  on set as they needed which which is  which is amazing and i'm sure you're all  doing fine but from from an audience  point of view  um i've i felt that there were parts of  yourself that um  were given to us and hopefully we we  will be the custodians of that  performance thank you very very much to  so  um sheila i i have to  um you know with with with your with  with  with mabel with with the character and  with with mental health  and with postnatal and  all the other um traumas that um  i think are encapsulated within within  your story  um how did you feel  you know when people finally got to  understand what went down  um that you you wanted to  project your maple you know  how because having met you and then  seeing the performance i was just like  oh my goodness she again took it to a  place that i'm  i didn't i didn't recognize sheila at  him i recognized this  incredibly caring maternal person that  was born in a time  where all of those um instincts  that she has were were not needed  or were needed but but she had to find a  way to hide them  to help others how did you did you work  your way into this character  oh yeah so i read the book um  at the time which i was cast i don't  believe the the  script for that episode was fully  finished was it barry  so i was like cool i was going to read  the book anyway but i read the book  um and very much in the same way in the  in the  the television series mabel is kind of  threaded through but then there is a  chapter  that kind of explains what happened to  her so that's very much a parallel  that's been carried over  into the show and so for me my my  process was quite self-contained  um in that it's all gonna happen in this  one episode  um and also everything happens in a  flashback so  mine and the rest of the world of the  present of the show doesn't really cross  over  um so for me it was just about i  couldn't really  be too focused on what was happening in  the rest of the show  if that means or my impact or mabel's  impact  in the rest of the story um and so for  me i was just focusing on  mabel's relationship with polly who  is the the character who who gives birth  and loses her child  um and the process was really  myself and barry tracking mabel's  journey throughout that episode so one  of the things that barry said is you  know mabel's got cracks  and we have to just track the cracks  particularly when you're shooting  slightly out of cm 